Castilian Spanish has a phoneme /=CE=B8/ which is written "z" or, before "e=
"=20
or "i", "c". Andalusian and American Spanishes have merged this phoneme=20
with /s/, though it is still distinct in morphology, in particular the "zc"=
=20
alterations of some 2nd and 3rd conjugation verbs. [=C3=B0] occurs in Spani=
sh as=20
an allophone of /d/, and maybe in Castilian of /=CE=B8/ (both "z" nor "s" h=
ave=20
voiced allophones, but I've never been in Castile to hear how they pronounc=
e,=20
e.g., "liderazgo" versus "liderad go...").

Some Hebrew dialects have [=CE=B8] and [=C3=B0] as morpho-allophones of /t/=
 and /d/,=20
distinguished in writing by the lack of a dagesh. Ashkenazis pronounce this=
=20
allophone as [s], saying e.g. "Shabbos".

